+(def ^:private display-type-note
+  "How the property operations write a block's type (patch 0037), shared by every one that sets it."
+  (str "A code, math or quote block: set logseq.property.node/display-type (or \"Node Display Type\") to \"code\", \"math\" or \"quote\" (\":code\" also works; trimmed, any case); anything else is refused, as is any other keyword property given text (only display-type can be set as text)."
+       " Setting it adds the matching class tag (Code, Math or Quote), removes the other two, removes lang unless the type is code, and removes the block's heading."
+       " logseq.property.code/lang is a code block's language (1 to 40 characters with no spaces, such as python or mermaid; case kept) and needs the block's type to be code once the call has applied."
+       " Both apply to blocks only: a page, tag, property or asset is refused."
+       " Code, Math and Quote can't be set as tags (use display-type); a block that holds one keeps it, and re-sending it is a no-op."
+       " A text block whose stored title holds \\# escapes from earlier writes keeps them when it becomes code or math, and the response carries a warning: to store the title verbatim, call updateBlock with the title and display-type together."
+       " Every write is read back in its transaction; one that didn't store what was asked fails the whole call."))
+
 (def ^:private today-note
   "Today is the date in the time zone the server is configured with (its TIMEZONE setting, an IANA zone such as Pacific/Auckland), or in UTC when none is set.")
 
 (def ^:private heading-note
   "How a written block title's markdown heading is stored, shared by every operation that writes one."
   (str "A title starting with # to ###### and whitespace (## Section) is stored as the app stores a heading: the title without the prefix, and the level in logseq.property/heading, which getBlock and getBlocks show as a property and getPageOutline shows as the ## prefix (listPageBlocks titles don't show it)."
-       " On update, a title without a prefix keeps the block's heading, and a code, math or quote block (one with a display type) keeps its title as given, # lines included, with no heading. Seven or more #, or # with nothing after it, is not a heading and is stored as text with the first # escaped (\\#)."))
+       " On update, a title without a prefix keeps the block's heading. Seven or more #, or # with nothing after it, is not a heading and is stored as text with the first # escaped (\\#)."
+       " A code, math or quote block (one with a display type) has no heading: its # lines are kept as given."))
+
+(def ^:private typed-block-note
+  "How the tree and update operations write a code, math or quote block (patch 0037)."
+  (str "display-type (code, math or quote) and lang (a code block's language) can be given on each block, or as the same properties (logseq.property.node/display-type or \"Node Display Type\", logseq.property.code/lang); given in both, the values must agree. An unknown value, lang without display-type code, or two different values is refused before anything is written, naming the node's path on the tree operations. A child doesn't take its parent's type."
+       " A code or math title is stored exactly as sent, judged on the block's type after the write (the call's display-type, else the stored one): no # is escaped and no inline #tag or [[link]] is resolved, so it adds no tags, refs or pages. A quote title keeps tag and link handling."
+       " A text block whose whole title is one fence becomes a code or math block, as the app does, unless convert-fences=false: ```lang … ``` becomes a code block holding the lines inside, with that lang (```mermaid is highlighted code; the app draws no diagram), and $$…$$ a math block; the response marks the block converted: fence. Text before or after the fence, two fences and an unclosed fence stay text. An explicit display-type or lang that disagrees with the fence is refused, naming the node. ```query fences stay text (a Query block is not supported yet) and are listed under kept with their path and reason, as is a fence whose language is longer than 40 characters. On update, a block that is already code, math or quote is never converted: its title is stored as sent under its type and lang, fence lines included. E2EE titles are never converted."))
 
 (def ^:private operation-docs
   {"listGraphs" ["List available graphs" "Returns cursor-paginated graphs available to the authenticated user, including E2EE-encrypted graphs flagged with graph-e2ee. Use the optional exact name filter to resolve a graph name to its UUID."]
    "listPages" ["List pages" "Returns a cursor-paginated list of page blocks in the graph."]
    "createPage" ["Create a page" "Creates a page block with the supplied title. Refuses with 409 and writes nothing when a page, journal, tag or property already uses the title, compared case-insensitively as Logseq compares page names; the error names each one's kind, title and UUID, so there is no need to search before creating. A page in the Recycle bin is restored instead. Not checked on E2EE graphs, whose names are ciphertext."]
    "listPageBlocks" ["List a page's blocks" "Returns a cursor-paginated list of the page's top-level blocks, including each selected block's descendant tree. Titles are stored text: a heading's level is not in them (getBlock or getPageOutline shows it)."]
-   "getPageOutline" ["Read a page as an outline" "Returns a page with its tags and property values, plus its whole block tree as a markdown outline: page and block references appear as [[titles]] instead of UUIDs, task status as [Status], a heading as its markdown prefix (## Section), and other block property values (user and library properties, and built-ins the editor shows such as Priority and Deadline) as indented key:: value lines. Large pages are truncated (see truncated and block-count). Unavailable for E2EE graphs."]
+   "getPageOutline" ["Read a page as an outline" "Returns a page with its tags and property values, plus its whole block tree as a markdown outline: page and block references appear as [[titles]] instead of UUIDs, task status as [Status], a heading as its markdown prefix (## Section), a code block as a ```lang fence, a math block as $$…$$ and a quote block as > lines (none with a heading prefix), and other block property values (user and library properties, and built-ins the editor shows such as Priority and Deadline) as indented key:: value lines. Large pages are truncated (see truncated and block-count). Unavailable for E2EE graphs."]
    "listPageReferences" ["List references to a page" "Returns a cursor-paginated flat list of blocks and pages that reference the addressed page. Results are not recursively expanded."]
    "getPageBacklinks" ["Show how a page is used" "Returns everything that points at a page, tag or property, grouped by relation with a complete count for each: references (blocks and pages whose text links it), tagged (objects tagged with it), property-values (entities with a property whose value is it, or whose text value links it, each with that property) and other (references Logseq records for other reasons, such as using it as a property key). Each group lists at most limit items, most recently updated first; total counts distinct entities. Use listPageReferences or listTagObjects to page through a whole group. Unavailable for E2EE graphs."]
    "getGraphHealth" ["Check graph health" "Runs graph-health checks for a periodic review and returns, per check, a complete count and the first limit items in title order (mismatched-choice-values in holder order): orphan-tags (user tags nothing is tagged with and no tag extends), orphan-properties (user properties without any value), duplicate-titles (pages, journals, tags and properties whose titles are equal ignoring case), pages-without-summary (non-journal user pages whose first top-level block does not start with summary-prefix), pages-without-tags (non-journal user pages with no tag) and mismatched-choice-values (values that are not one of their property's own choices: another property's choice with the same title, on a property with choices or on a user property without them, such as a node property, reason another-propertys-choice; something other than a choice, reason not-a-choice; or the property's own choice that the holder's tags do not allow, reason out-of-scope. Values cleared to Logseq's empty placeholder are left out; each item names the holder, the property, the value, the property the value's choice belongs to and the reason). created-after and updated-after narrow the two page checks. To page through one check, pass it alone in checks with its next-cursor. Unavailable for E2EE graphs."]
